Proposition 2.1(iii). Inserting a token that is depth-entailed by a finset preserves depth consistency of that finset.

The argument is strong induction on the maximum of the depth of the finset and the depth of the token. Bottom uses preservation of depth consistency under insertion of bottom, and an atom uses preservation under insertion of an entailed atom. For a function token, the inserted finset lies in the function world and still has an empty atom projection. Its function tokens are the new token, whose components are consistent by the entailment, or old tokens, which remain well formed. A selection containing the new token is split by filtering that token out. Entailments of the witness inputs are weakened onto the combined input, the depth bounds stay below the induction parameter, and the inductive insertion hypothesis gives depth consistency of that union. Monotonicity and the function clause of the original finset give depth consistency of the combined outputs; the output entailments are weakened in the same way, and a second inductive union identifies the output of the selection. A selection that omits the new token lies in the original function-token projection, so the original function clause applies.
